[Note "In the context of chess endgames, particularly when the defending King is trailing behind a passed pawn, securing a win typically requires a precise maneuver known as cutting off the King. This strategy involves positioning your rook along the fourth rank—counted from the defender's perspective—to create an impassable horizontal barrier. By establishing this control, you effectively isolate the defending King, preventing it from crossing the board's midpoint to support its own pieces or block the pawn's advance, thereby converting a positional advantage into a decisive victory. The result of this position hinges entirely on a question of timing and coordination: specifically, whether the White King can successfully occupy the d2 square at the precise moment the pawn advances to the second rank. This maneuver is critical because the King’s presence on d2 is required to support the pawn's progress and prevent the opponent from establishing a blockade. If it is currently White's turn to move, they possess the necessary initiative to win this race, allowing the King to arrive exactly when needed to secure the square and guarantee the pawn's eventual promotion."]

