[Event "#26 Rook Endgames"]
[Site "This is a draw"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White ""]
[Black ""]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4k3/R7/8/3KP3/8/8/8/7r b - - 0 1"]
[Rating ""]
[Note "THE PHILIDOR POSITION. The critical principles of the Rook and Pawn versus Rook endgame are vividly demonstrated by this analysis, which outlines the definitive drawing technique discovered by Philidor in the eighteenth century. The defense rests on a rigid two-part strategy: initially, the defending King blockades the pawn while the Rook patrols the third rank to cut off the attacking King, preventing it from stepping in front of its pawn to clear a path. However, the moment the pawn advances to challenge this blockade, the defensive setup shifts; the Rook immediately transfers to the rear of the board to launch a barrage of checks from behind, forcing a draw because the advancing King has lost the ability to use its own pawn as a shelter against the attack."]

*

[Event "#27"]
[Site "White mates in 18 moves"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White ""]
[Black ""]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4K3/4P1k1/8/8/8/8/3r4/5R2 w - - 0 1"]
[Rating ""]
[Note "The Lucena Position is the cornerstone of winning rook endgames, arising when the defending King is completely cut off from the promotion path and cannot physically block the pawn. Because the defender's King is out of play, the burden of defense falls entirely on their Rook, which attempts to imprison the attacking King directly in front of its own pawn; as long as the monarch blocks the queening square, the pawn cannot advance. To break this deadlock, the attacking side executes a maneuver famously known as 'building a bridge': they position their own Rook—typically on the fourth rank—to act as a future shield. This preparation allows the attacking King to finally step out of the pawn’s way, because when the defending Rook inevitably delivers checks to drive the King back, the attacking Rook can interpose itself, blocking the line of fire and guaranteeing the pawn’s safe promotion."]

