[Note "However, if it is Black to move, the situation becomes drastically different, effectively condemning the White king to"life imprisonment"behind its own pawn. This deadlock highlights a fundamental limitation of the piece that we observed earlier: a knight, by its very nature, is incapable of"losing a tempo"or triangulating in the same manner as a king or queen. To secure a win in this position, White simply needs to transfer the obligation to move to Black, which would force the defending king to step aside and allow White’s king to escape the corner. Yet, because the knight always changes the color of the square it stands on with every move, it cannot simply waste time to hand the turn back to the opponent without altering the essential structure of the position. Since this crucial transfer of tempo is impossible to execute, the White king remains permanently barricaded, and the game concludes as a theoretical draw. To navigate this specific endgame scenario with confidence, the reader can rely on a precise theoretical rule that allows for a quick assessment of the position without the need for exhaustive calculation. The guideline establishes that the weaker side can successfully secure a draw if they are able to maneuver their king to a square on the first or second rank of the bishop’s file, provided that this square is of the same color as the one currently occupied by the opposing knight. This geometric relationship is the key to the defense; since a knight is mechanically incapable of attacking a square of the same color as the one it stands on, occupying such a square creates a temporary safe harbor that disrupts the attacker's coordination and prevents them from forcing a win."]

