[Note "White has pressed the advantage to its absolute limit, yet the game remains a draw because it is impossible to completely paralyze the defending Knight; moves like ...Nf8+ or ...Nb8 allow Black to keep the piece active enough to hold the defense. This escape is significant because, as a general rule, the edge of the board is hostile to Knights, severely restricting their available squares and making them vulnerable to being trapped. While Black survives in this specific setup, that theoretical 'edge weakness' is often fatal in similar positions—such as endings with a Knight’s pawn on the sixth rank—where the attacker can successfully exploit the board's boundary to dominate the restricted Knight and force a win."]

