[Rating ""]
[Note "When the defending King is already positioned directly in the pawn's path, securing a draw is a natural and straightforward result, provided the Queen helps obstruct the attack. This safety relies on the principle of liquidation: if the attacker tries to force progress and cover their King from checks, they are often compelled to interpose their own Queen, leading to a trade of pieces. Since the defending King already occupies the critical blockading square, the resulting King-and-pawn ending is a theoretical draw. Consequently, the most instructive positions for study are not these static blockades, but rather the sharp scenarios where the defending King is cut off from the action, placing the entire burden of stopping the pawn solely on the Queen's ability to maneuver and check. Although the Black Queen successfully halts the pawn's advance, its current placement on g8 is fundamentally passive, serving as a static blockade rather than an active threat. To convert this advantage into a win, White must systematically maneuver to dislodge the Queen from this critical square; however, this task demands extreme precision, as any careless move could allow the Black Queen to break the blockade and launch a relentless series of checks, saving the game through a perpetual check defense."]

